  }
}
```


```json
{
  "tool": "run_shell",
  "args": {
    "cmd": "cd code && python analyze_datasets.py"
  }
}
```

Good! I can see the label counts are very small (around 30 samples total for each dataset based on the counts shown). Wait, that seems too small. Let me check the actual size of the CSV files. The registry says "train_patches" but those numbers are in the hundreds (400-2200). Let me check the actual row count.

Actually, looking at the label counts: {0: 7, 1: 6, 2: 5, 3: 12} for D0014 totals 30 samples, but the registry says 2080 train patches. This suggests the CSV files might only show a preview or the actual data is much larger. Let me check the full size of one CSV file.

```json
{
  "tool": "run_shell",
  "args": {
    "cmd": "wc -l ../data/patches/D0014/train.csv"
  }
}
```


```json
{
  "tool": "run_shell",
  "args": {
    "cmd": "head -5 ../data/patches/D0014/train.csv"
  }
}
```


```json
{
  "tool": "run_shell",
  "args": {
    "cmd": "tail -5 ../data/patches/D0014/train.csv"
  }
}
```

Let me use Python to check the file size. I'll write a quick script.

```json
{
  "tool": "write_file",
  "args": {
    "path": "code/check_data_size.py",
    "content": "import pandas as pd\nimport os\n\n# Check a few datasets\nfor ds_id in ['D0014', 'D0011', 'D0006', 'D0003']:\n    train_path = f\"../data/patches/{ds_id}/train.csv\"\n    val_path = f\"../data/patches/{ds_id}/val.csv\"\n    test_path = f\"../data/patches/{ds_id}/test.csv\"\n    \n    for split, path in [('train', train_path), ('val', val_path), ('test', test_path)]:\n        if os.path.exists(path):\n            df = pd.read_csv(path)\n            print(f\"Dataset {ds_id} {split}: {len(df)} rows, {df.shape[1]} columns\")\n            print(f\"  Label distribution: {dict(df['label'].value_counts().sort_index())}\")\n        else:\n            print(f\"Dataset {ds_id} {split}: File not found\")\n    print()"
  }
}
```


```json
{
  "tool": "run_shell",
  "args": {
    "cmd": "cd code && python check_data_size.py"
  }
}
```

